{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788082552,"nonce":"J6gZCV1VepwZdv7kLzYG2JN/PvQ=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABO2P7Qw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/124","origin":"github"},"title":"jno.core rejects a parameter-only fit spanning two solves as an ambiguous domain","message":"A data-misfit loss over **two `fem.solve()` calls on two different meshes** cannot be built:\n\n```\nValueError: Cannot infer domain: constraints reference 2 distinct domains\n([\u003cPolygonDomain ...\u003e, \u003cPolygonDomain ...\u003e]). All constraints must share a single domain.\n```\n\n### Repro\n\n```python\nalpha  = jno.np.parameter((1,), name=\"alpha\")          # shared unknown\ncoarse = poisson(alpha, mesh_size=0.3)                 # two solves,\nfine   = poisson(alpha, mesh_size=0.2)                 # two meshes\n\nloss = (coarse.solve() - obs_c).mse + (fine.solve() - obs_f).mse\njno.core([loss])                                       # \u003c-- raises\n```\n\n### Why it is wrong\n\n`_infer_domain_from_constraints` collects every `_domain` reachable in the constraint tree and\ndemands a unique one. That conflates two different kinds of reference:\n\n* a `Variable` / `TensorTag` is a **collocation** reference — core draws sample coordinates from it;\n* a solve node`s `_domain` (`fem.solve()`, `jno.fdm([...]).solve()`) is merely the mesh its PDE is\n  discretized on.\n\nAn inverse loss reads **no coordinate** from that mesh: its Variables live inside the solve and are\nconsumed by the assembler. So the loss above collocates on neither domain, which domain drives the\nepoch loop cannot matter, and there is nothing ambiguous to reject.\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is the shape of any coupled forward chain (e.g. process -\u003e device, where each stage is solved\non its own mesh), so that class of inverse problem cannot be expressed at all.\n\nThe single-solve case already works — a `_trivial_domain()` fallback has been in place since #87 —\nbut the workaround it replaced is still widely present: a placeholder\n`jno.domain.from_array({\"_\": np.zeros((1, 1))})` appears in 13 test files, 3 tutorial scripts and\none published doc page, and is the only way through the multi-solve case today.\n\n### Also\n\nThe accompanying `\"No domain Variable found in the constraints\"` warning fires on every *correct*\nparameter-only inverse problem, advising the user to add a Variable they do not want.","files":null}]}